"""Corporate accountability and community monitoring."""


def build(course, module):
    return course(103, 'Corporate Accountability and Community Monitoring',
        'Corporate\nAccountability and\nCommunity Monitoring',
        'Trace an extractive-sector commitment to an authorised record, independently assess delivery and propose accountable follow-up.', [
        module('Read disclosed records in context',
            'Distinguish different kinds of disclosures and check their scope, date and decision relevance.',
            """Transparency can make company and government actions easier to question. A licence record identifies a formal authorisation within a stated scope. A company register may identify a legal entity. A beneficial ownership record concerns who ultimately owns or controls an entity under the applicable reporting rules. A payment record describes a reported transaction. Each record has a different purpose, and a reader needs to know which question the record can answer. Compare relevant fields for identity, dates and scope before drawing a conclusion. [S88]

For extractive work, ask whether a social contribution arises under a legal or contractual commitment or from a discretionary decision. A publicly announced commitment may be recorded by more than one institution, each using different dates or definitions. The EITI requirements distinguish categories of disclosure and how participating countries report them. A missing number should be labelled missing until its meaning is checked. The NEITI solid minerals audit index can guide learners towards official reports, including historical disclosures, once the appropriate reporting period and coverage are checked. [S88] [S91]

Start an evidence log with the source issuing body, record date, period, field and limitation. If one document concerns payment by a company and another concerns receipt by a local body, treat them as different observations until the records can be reconciled. Use the disclosure to formulate a question rather than drawing a conclusion about delivery from only one entry. Request clarification through the appropriate authority. [S88]

The Guiding Principles link reporting to due diligence and remediation. For a practitioner, good reporting helps people see what is changing and access the appropriate remedy. Include information people can act on and protect records that could reveal sensitive case details. [S86]""",
            'An announcement says a road fund was paid. The audit file shows a commitment, an older disbursement and no record of the current receipt.',
            'Identify what each record establishes. Write a short reconciliation request with the record period, entity and missing confirmation.',
            'Separate commitment, outgoing disbursement and verified receipt. Check date, scope and entity before reporting a gap or outcome.',
            """
Match source and claim. | Which record helps identify who ultimately controls a reporting extractive entity? | A relevant beneficial ownership record. | A training attendance sheet. | A notice of community meeting results. | A supplier delivery receipt alone. | Ownership and control information answers a specific transparency question.
Distinguish contribution types. | What should be verified before describing a project payment as mandatory? | The applicable legal or contractual basis for the payment. | Whether the announcement used a formal banner. | Whether the payment was discussed at a meeting. | Whether the payment amount was rounded. | The obligation depends on the governing requirement.
Read time scope. | A 2023 audit amount and a 2025 programme receipt differ. What is the first review step? | Check period, entity and definition before attempting reconciliation. | Treat the difference as confirmed missing funds. | Copy the larger figure into both records. | Assume the same line is being counted twice. | Like-for-like comparison is needed to interpret the difference.
Distinguish missing and zero. | A field is blank in a disclosure table. How should it be recorded in a learner evidence log? | Missing or unspecified pending clarification. | Automatically zero for every entity. | Positive because the project announced spending. | Exempt under any possible standard. | A blank has an unknown meaning until verified.
Tie reporting to action. | Which public description helps people pursue a delayed benefit? | Scope, expected date, what has been verified and an appropriate follow-up route. | Only the programme logo and annual total. | Private payment details for unrelated households. | The name of the press release author without details. | Practical information connects a disclosure with follow-up.
"""),
        module('Monitor delivery and distribution',
            'Define indicators and verification routes that distinguish resources committed from lived results.',
            """Start with a commitment and describe the benefit as a sequence. A work order, a purchase and an opening date are process milestones. Accessible use by the intended residents is a result that requires its own measure. The monitoring team should state the population, comparison period, unit and practical condition for a successful use. Give the measure a clear owner and explain which evidence can corroborate it. Community contributors can help describe the condition that matters, such as operating before the first shift. [S88]

Build a simple record of planned, approved, delivered and verified stages. Suppose a project approves service for 24 workers and 18 try it. If 12 can use the service routinely, report the fraction of the full approved group and of those who tried it. Record whether each unsuccessful visit reflects capacity, timing, affordability or another barrier. Keep process and result measures separate so a public account can show where delivery is falling short. [S89]

Ask how patterns differ across affected groups and locations. Research on mining-related perceived ecosystem losses in Ghana illustrates why a summary count can miss changed access, household work or cultural practice. An indicator that uses the average across all residents may hide a decline among a group that depended heavily on a service. Seek input from people least able to join the primary monitoring activity and describe limitations proportionately. Preserve privacy by presenting safe aggregates and retaining a protected route for relevant reviewers. [R99]

Agree how discrepancies will be handled. Keep dated accounts with their sources, including cases that contradict a positive picture. Refer physical testing to competent teams and avoid unapproved site access. Define who can make a correction and when to recheck the outcome.""",
            'An agreement offers 24 workers a service, 18 try it and 12 use it routinely. The contractor reports 100 percent delivery.',
            'Calculate two meaningful rates. Define a corroborating record and an action that addresses an observed timing or affordability barrier.',
            'Report 50 percent of the approved group and about 67 percent of those who tried it. Verify why planned access did not translate into use.',
            """
Map the chain. | Which stage shows a programme is available as advertised? | Confirmed operation at the stated places and times. | The line item was proposed. | The budget was discussed. | A description appeared in a draft. | Operations require evidence beyond planning.
Calculate routine use. | Twelve of 24 approved workers use a service routinely. What percentage of those approved use it? | 50 percent. | About 67 percent. | 75 percent. | 100 percent. | The approved-group denominator is twenty-four.
Track causes of failure. | Some participants arrive after a service closes because of shift hours. How should the monitor record this? | A timing barrier by affected group and relevant attempts. | As universal lack of motivation. | As successful attendance because attempts were made. | As a budget overrun without evidence. | Recording the failure mechanism supports a specific correction.
Check corroboration. | The service provider reports successful opening every weekday. Which is a useful independent comparison? | Dated access reports from an appropriately reached sample of users. | A logo included in the contract. | The amount written in a press notice. | Meeting attendance by procurement staff. | Independent experience helps test the reported pattern.
Protect community contributions. | Publishing exact comments could reveal identities in a small work crew. What should the monitoring team do? | Preserve a protected record and publish an appropriately aggregated pattern. | Publish the full notes with times and names. | Exclude the crew from every analysis. | Change the dates in the source records to conceal them. | Safe reporting can retain evidence without exposing identities.
"""),
        module('Request correction and verify accountable action',
            'Prepare a proportionate evidence-based accountability request and specify how correction will be verified.',
            """An accountability request should describe a specific gap and the change the responsible body can make. State the commitment or relevant expectation, the current evidence and who appears affected. Include the area and reporting period and separate observations from open questions. Share a method for providing corrections. Give the responsible body a reasonable opportunity to respond through appropriate channels and preserve access to independent review. Do not present an unexplained discrepancy as conclusive wrongdoing. [S86] [S88]

Use an action register. For each item, set an accountable role, an action, a target date and evidence that would show completion. An answer can acknowledge the concern, contest part of it or commit to a change. Keep these outcomes visible. For a delay in community transport, a proposed new schedule is progress. Verification would require checking that it actually operates and meets the needs of the intended users. Give the community a way to comment on the revised evidence. [S89]

Make a distinction between public reporting and protected case handling. If the request concerns a pattern of access failures, send a minimum supporting summary and share sensitive evidence only under appropriate authority. Review who could be harmed by public disclosure. A reliable grievance channel or independent escalation route can support people who cannot safely attach their names to a public request. Keep records that let an authorised reviewer trace what changed, even if the public summary is aggregated. [S86]

Document whether the correction worked across the intended reach. Identify continuing barriers and reserve a subsequent review. If a result is disputed, acknowledge that the evidence is contested and record how it will be resolved or reviewed.""",
            'An operator disputes a low service-use figure and offers a new schedule. It asks the team to close the case before checking the schedule.',
            'Write a two-paragraph request with a source boundary, named corrective owner, verification condition and review date.',
            'Record the disagreement and keep the measure open until the revised route operates and intended users can use it.',
            """
Write a scoped request. | Which request is easiest for a provider to verify? | Compare the signed operating window with dated missed trips for the same route. | Resolve every problem in the region at once. | Confirm that all records are incorrect. | Explain unrelated spending before identifying the programme. | Bounded requests identify the record and action at issue.
Handle disputed evidence. | Two credible sources conflict about when the service opened. What belongs in the request? | Both dated claims and the evidence needed to resolve them. | Only the account that supports the current narrative. | An averaged opening date presented as verified. | A finding of wrongdoing based on disagreement. | Preserve provenance and uncertainty until the conflict is resolved.
Assign the correction. | Who should own a committed timetable correction? | The responsible operator or authority able to change it. | The person who reported the original failure by default. | Everyone in the community jointly without a role. | The author of a general policy paper. | Assign an action to a role that can carry it out.
Verify effectiveness. | A new timetable is printed. What supports closing the access item? | Evidence the service follows it and the affected users can reach and use it. | Type size is larger. | A project memo repeats the proposed hours. | An administrator files the draft schedule. | Implementation and use require their own verification.
Use protected escalation. | An unresolved pattern includes reports of reprisal. What follow-up is suitable? | Use a protected referral or independent route with the minimum necessary information. | Attach every named account to a public notice. | Ask complainants to confront the named person in a group. | Delete all evidence to close the dashboard item. | A safe route keeps sensitive evidence available for the right reviewer.
"""),
    ])
